The fog rolled in thick as wool, swallowing the cliffs whole, and the lantern's beam cut a thin, trembling line across the black sea. Thomas Whitaker, the keeper, trudged up the spiral stairs to the lantern room, his boots echoing against stone. On the battered wooden desk lay a single envelope, its paper yellowed at the edges, the seal cracked but intact. He frowned, turning it over; the ink was unmistakably his own, the slant of the letters familiar, yet the date printed in the corner read 'October 12, 2035.' A chill brushed his neck as he lifted the flap, the paper whispering against his fingertips. He stared at the familiar script, the words waiting, unspoken, as the wind howled outside.

